# 30. Interview-Level Architect Answer

If an interviewer asks:

### "Which architecture would you choose?"

Avoid answering:

> "I would use microservices."

A stronger Staff/Architect answer is:

> "I would first understand the business capabilities, domain boundaries, scalability requirements, availability and consistency requirements, team topology, deployment model, security requirements, operational maturity, cost constraints, and expected evolution of the system. Based on those constraints, I would choose the simplest architecture that satisfies the requirements.
>
> For example, I may start with a modular monolith using DDD and Clean/Hexagonal Architecture. If specific business capabilities require independent scaling, deployment, ownership, or fault isolation, I would extract those capabilities into microservices. For asynchronous communication I would consider event-driven architecture, CQRS where read/write characteristics justify it, and Saga for distributed business workflows.
>
> The goal is not to use the most sophisticated architecture; the goal is to choose an architecture whose benefits justify its complexity and operational cost."

